              },
              {
                "description": "Specifies the policy used to decide which transactions are allowed to execute in parallel on read replicas. Setting this parameter to DATABASE enables database-level MTS. Setting this parameter to LOGICAL_CLOCK enables transaction-level MTS. Database-level MTS is a feature in MySQL 5.6.36. The value of this parameter takes effect only when the value of slave_preserve_commit_order is OFF. However, the default value of slave_preserve_commit_order is ON in 5.7 earlier versions. If the value fails to be changed, manually upgrade the MySQL version. (Supported in versions later than 5.6.40 or 5.7)",
                "name": "slave_parallel_type",
                "readonly": false,
                "restart_required": true,
                "type": "string",
                "value": "LOGICAL_CLOCK",
                "value_range": "LOGICAL_CLOCK|DATABASE"
              },
              {
                "description": "For transaction-level Multi-Threaded Slave (MTS) replication, enabling this parameter ensures that transactions are executed and committed on the read replica in the same order as they appear in the read replica's relay log (the same order as they applied on the primary instance). Setting this parameter has no effect on the read replicas for which the MTS option is not enabled. Enabling MTS for a read replica ensures that transactions are executed on the replica in the same order as they are applied on the primary instance. In addition, if MTS is not enabled, checking for recently executed transactions does not guarantee that all previous transactions from the primary instance have been executed on the read replica. (Supported in versions later than 5.6.40 or 5.7, In versions later than 8.0.26, this parameter is equivalent to replica_preserve_commit_order.)",
                "name": "slave_preserve_commit_order",
